with a border: as, to ''border'' a garment or a garden.
{{smaller block|Rivulets ''bordered'' with the softest grass.
{{right|''T. Warton'', Hist. Eng. Poetry.}}}}
'''2.''' To form a border or boundary to.—'''3.''' To lie on the border of; be contiguous to; adjoin; lie next.
{{smaller block|Sheba and Raamah ''border'' the Persian Gulf. {{right|''Raleigh.''}}}}
'''4†.''' To confine or keep within bounds; limit.
{{smaller block|<poem>That nature, which contemns its origin,
Cannot be ''border’d'' certain in itself.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Lear, iv. 2.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To have a contiguous boundary or dividing line; abut exteriorly: with ''on'' or ''upon'': as, the United States ''border on'' the two great oceans.
{{smaller block|Virtue and Honour had their temples ''bordering on'' each other and are sometimes both on the same coin.
{{right|''Addison'', Dialogues on Medals, ii.}}}}
{{smaller|'''To border on''' or '''upon''', figuratively, to approach closely in character; verge on; resemble closely: as, his conduct ''borders upon'' vulgarity.}}
{{smaller block|Wit which ''borders upon'' profaneness . . . deserves to be branded as folly. ''Tillotson'', Works (ed. 1728), I. 33.}}

'''bordered''' (bôr′dėrd), ''p. a.'' [< ''border'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having a border: specifically, in ''math.'', applied to a determinant formed from another by adding one or more rows and columns. {{smaller|Thus, a ''bordered'' symmetrical determinant is a determinant formed by adding a row and column to a symmetrical determinant.}}

